title: The TLS read path has no automated coverage
updated: 2026-08-28T00:26:18Z
---

t/tls.t covers option assembly and certificate handling; nothing in the suite
reads a byte over TLS. The transport's reading was rebuilt in karr #60 and the
TLS branch of it was verified by hand, against a local IO::Socket::SSL server
with a self-signed certificate -- outside the suite, so nothing keeps it
verified.

MEASURED by hand during #60, before and after, identical both ways:

  three writes 0.15s apart -> 3 on_chunk calls at 0.16s / 0.31s / 0.46s
  100000 bytes over many records -> 7 calls, 100000 bytes, none lost
  delivered, then silent, read_timeout 1 -> Error::Timeout after 1.07s,
    phase read, summary delivered=1, the bytes already with the callback

Those three are what a test would assert. What makes them worth automating is
that TLS is the one transport where a short read is the normal case -- one
plaintext record per sysread -- so the "a short read is not an end of stream"
rule has no margin there, and IO::Socket::SSL's own read/sysread split
(ssl_read_all on a blocking socket vs a single Net::SSLeay::read) is exactly
the distinction #60 depends on.

Costs a self-signed certificate and a forked server. Both are cheap, but the
file would be the first in t/ to need either, so it is a decision rather than
an addition: gate it on IO::Socket::SSL being installed (it is a recommended,
not a required, dependency) and skip_all without it.

Related: #60.
